  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a retrospective case-control design in a single county, meaning the observed protective effects of greenness may not generalize to other geographic regions with different urban layouts or socioeconomic dynamics.",
      "what": "A matched case-control study in Cook County, Illinois, analyzed 899 firearm-related homicides and 2,256 natural deaths to evaluate the protective effects of urban vegetation. The study measured greenness using satellite-derived NDVI, tree canopy coverage, park proximity, and two machine learning-derived metrics from Google Street View images. The green view index from street-level imagery (OR = 0.693), walkable street greenness (OR = 0.787), and tree canopy coverage (OR = 0.792) were all significantly associated with lower odds of firearm homicide.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the protective association of urban greenness against violent crime is highly dependent on neighborhood socioeconomic context, showing that tree canopy and segmented green space provide substantially stronger protective effects in less deprived areas."
    }
